  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 151 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLow clouds/stratus have spread into some SW MT valleys including \nareas as far south as the Gallatin Valley and Norris Hill. These \nareas may see some improvement through the rest of this afternoon \nwhile light easterly surface winds across the north-central MT \nplains will maintain widespread low clouds/stratus along with some \nlingering areas of fog. Areas of fog will redevelop/expand again \ntonight across the plains while increasing mid-high level clouds may \nprevent widespread fog development overnight across southwest MT. A \nmore southerly flow aloft develops tonight ahead of an upper level \ntrough offshore of the Pacific NW with a few light showers \npotentially tracking north into portions of SW MT and along the \nContinental Divide after midnight.   \n\nAdditional showers track northward into primarily areas near the ID \nborder through much of the day tomorrow as the offshore trough \nelongates and shifts inland. More widespread precipitation develops \nlate Saturday night through Sunday as the trough axis shifts east \nacross the Rockies. The bulk of the energy tracks south of the area \nwith highest precip amounts expected across eastern portions of \nsouthwest MT while a more brief period of precipitation shifts across \ncentral/north-central MT, mainly late Sunday, before the trough \nand associated precipitation shift east of the area on Monday. \nSnow levels are initially above 7000 ft with models continuing to \ntrend slower with the arrival of cooling aloft and lowering snow \nlevels on Sunday, limiting impactful snowfall to areas mainly \nabove passes through much of the weekend. \n\nUpper level ridging rebuilds early next week, but may be transient \nwith most model ensembles showing some form of undercutting energy \ntracking east from the Pacific and inland by Wednesday. \nTemperatures should rebound to closer to average for at least the \nfirst part of next week. Hoenisch\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nAreas of dense fog are likely again tonight with with the \neasterly/light-upslope flow supporting the highest risk (60-80%) for \nvisibility of 1/4 mile or less along areas adjacent to the east \nslopes of the Rocky Mtn Front and Judith/Snowy Ranges. The influx \nof mid-high level cloud cover from The south later tonight may \nhelp mitigate a greater coverage of dense fog across north-central\nMT.\n\nTemperatures in the mid-upper 30s across the north-central MT \nplains will fall to near or slightly below freezing again tonight, \nbringing some risk for localized icing of elevated surfaces, \nespecially in areas where fog is most dense and may support \nfreezing drizzle. The shallow cool airmass continues to erode \nSaturday and Sunday, but a few isolated locations may hold onto the \ncolder air as precipitation arrives Saturday night Sunday creating\nsome potential for light freezing rain/drizzle. \n\nThe risk for snow accumulation in excess of 2 inches late this \nweekend is highest at elevations above 7000 ft but only around 20% \nor less for most passes at or below 7000 ft. Hoenisch",
